wnloaded data.\n3. **Part 2-dry-experiment-3**: Normalize RNA sequencing data using DESeq2 and gcRMA.\n4. **Part 2-dry-experiment-4**: Document preprocessing steps.\n\n### Part 3: Differential Expression Analysis\n1. **Part 3-dry-experiment-1**: Load RNA sequencing data from TCGA and GSE202361.\n2. **Part 3-dry-experiment-2**: Create a design matrix for differential expression analysis.\n3. **Part 3-dry-experiment-3**: Fit linear model to the data using `lmFit`.\n4. **Part 3-dry-experiment-4**: Apply empirical Bayes statistics using `eBayes`.\n5. **Part 3-dry-experiment-5**: Perform differential expression analysis with `topTable`.\n\n### Part 4: Pathway Analysis and Therapeutic Targets\n1. **Part 4-dry-experiment-1**: Conduct Gene Ontology (GO) enrichment analysis.\n2. **Part 4-dry-experiment-2**: Perform KEGG pathway analysis on DEGs.\n3. **Part 4-dry-experiment-3**: Prepare a discussion document on potential therapeutic targets.\n\n### Part 5: Immune-Related lncRNA Analysis\n1. **Part 5-dry-experiment-1**: Acquire RNA sequencing data on immune checkpoint-related lncRNAs.\n2. **Part 5-dry-experiment-2**: Analyze expression of immune checkpoint-related lncRNAs.\n3. **Part 5-dry-experiment-3**: Perform survival analysis using Kaplan-Meier method.\n4. **Part 5-dry-experiment-4**: Investigate predictive value of lncRNAs for immunotherapy response.\n5. **Part 5-dry-experiment-5**: Document challenges faced during the analysis.\n\n---\n\n## File Contract\n\n- **Input Files**:\n  - RNA sequencing data (raw counts, FASTQ, or processed data from TCGA, GEO)\n  - Metadata (sample characteristics, gene annotations, patient information)\n  - Experimental design specifications (e.g., case-control matrix)\n  - Biobank sample lists and metadata\n\n- **Output Files**:\n  - **Part 1**:  \n    - `sample_list_final.csv`: A final list of eligible samples.\n    - `sample_statistics_summary.csv`: Summary statistics report.\n  - **Part 2**:  \n    - `qc_report.txt`: QC results from RNA d